 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT authorizing the conveyance of real property at the State University of\nNew York College at Delhi",
    "basePrintNo": "S6890",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S6890-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "",
    "lawSection": "Education",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S6890\n\nSPONSOR: MARTUCCI\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act authorizing the conveyance of real property at the State Univer-\nsity of New York College at Delhi\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo authorize the exchange of parcels of realty between the State of New\nYork, on behalf of the State University of New York Technical College at\nDelhi and the College Foundation at Delhi in order to facilitate the\nconstruction and operation of a hotel.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 of the legislation states the intent underscoring this legis-\nlation.\n\nSection 2 of the legislation provides that, notwithstanding the\nprovisions of any other law, the State of New York may convey to College\nFoundation at Delhi a parcel of real property, now owned or to be owned\nby the State,under the jurisdiction of SUNY Delhi, described in section\n3 of the legislation, in exchange for the conveyance by the College\nFoundation at Delhi to the State, for the benefit of SUNY Delhi, of four\nparcels of real property described in section 4 of the legislation, upon\nsuch terms and conditions as the parties may agree.\n\nSection 5 of the legislation provides that it shall take effect imme-\ndiately.\n\n \nEXISTING LAW:\n\nThe Public Lands Law (\"PLL\") governs the manner in which the State of\nNew York may convey title lands owned by the State. Once a State entity\ndetermines that land is no longer useful or necessary for the purposes\nof the State entity (PLL 30-A), the Commissioner of General Services is\nauthorized to sell the property at public auction (PLL 33(1) or to\nconvey title to a municipality for certain uses (PLL 34).\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThe purpose of this legislation is to authorize the exchange of parcels\nof real property between the State of New York and the College Founda-\ntion at Delhi to facilitate the construction and operation of a hotel in\nsupport of SUNY Delhi's hospitality management program. In absence of\nthe legislation, SUNY Delhi would lack authority to convey its parcel to\nthe College Foundation at Delhi since the provisions of the Public Lands\nLaw would otherwise apply.\n\nThe College Foundation at Delhi would acquire a 7.2-acre parcel, the\nBrown Athletic Fields, located on Main Street in the Village of Delhi.\nThis property is adjacent to the SUNY Delhi campus. It is currently used\nas an athletic field and will be developed for the site of a future\nhotel that will provide experiential learning opportunities for SUNY\nDelhi's Hospitality Management and Culinary academic programs. Further,\nthe hotel will provide substantial economic development benefit to\nvillage of Delhi, Delaware County, and the region. Following the\nexchange, the athletic fields will be relocated to another campus prop-\nerty. The legislation allows SUNY Delhi to exchange a parcel of land now\nowned by the State and under the jurisdiction of the SUNY Delhi for\nparcels of land of similar value owned by the College Foundation at\nDelhi. The parcels SUNY Delhi would be acquiring are: B-1 Route 10/Bell\nHill, a 180.19-acre parcel surrounding and contiguous to the SUNY Delhi\nmain campus a critical piece of property of future College growth/use;\nB-2 Golf Course Maintenance Property, a 6.86-acre property used as an\napplied learning laboratory for SUNY Delhi's Golf and Sports Turf\nManagement programs, this facility will also serve as an emergency shel-\nter for animals housed at the college farm (on SUNY Delhi's Valley\nCampus); B-3 Outdoor Education Center, a 38.2-acre property developed by\nthe college's applied sciences programs that serves as a learning labo-\nratory for those programs; and B-4 Arbor Hill Property, a 15.2-acre\nproperty in close proximity to SUNY Delhi's Valley Campus, will allow\nfor future growth and development of SUNY Delhi's Valley Campus and\nserve as a secondary relocation site for farm animals in the event of\nemergency.\n\nBoth parties consider such an exchange of property advantageous for\ntheir respective campus projects. The College Foundation at Delhi's\nparcels affords opportunities for integration of the academic program\ninclusion and will allow for future development of the SUNY Delhi\ncampus.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
